### 4.12 Admin Content Management (CMS) Module *(new)*

Lets non-technical staff (Content Editor role) manage everything in §4.11 through the Admin Console, with a Draft → Preview → Publish workflow.

| Feature | Description |
|---|---|
| Section Editor | Edit homepage sections (hero, stats, how-it-works, CTA, footer) via structured forms — not raw code |
| Blog Management | Create/edit/publish/unpublish/schedule posts; categories & tags; featured image; author byline |
| Testimonials Manager | Add/edit/remove/reorder testimonials |
| FAQ Manager | Add/edit/remove/reorder FAQ items; group by category |
| Media Library | Upload, browse, search, and reuse images/assets across the site |
| SEO Fields | Per-page and per-post meta title, meta description, OG image, slug |
| Draft/Preview/Publish | Edits save as drafts; preview against a staging URL before going live |
| Version History | See prior versions of a page/post; rollback (Phase 2 if not MVP) |
| Site Settings | Global contact info, social links, footer link groups |
| Admin RBAC | Content Editor vs Super Admin permission scoping |

**Architecture recommendation:** rather than a separate headless CMS vendor, model content as tables in the existing PostgreSQL database (`pages`, `sections`, `blog_posts`, `testimonials`, `faqs`, `media_assets`) and expose them through the same backend API, editable via the Admin Console. This keeps content, listings, and app data in one system, avoids an extra vendor dependency, and lets the public site and app share the same data layer where relevant (e.g., featured listings).

